# The Rust value wrapper's field is `UString`, not `String`

Resolving `tests/ts/value-type.test.ts:125` against the generator at
`boring-wt-architecture` revision `e1c6597514634fd347d392709793cc19bd96c9a2`.

**Decision: the generator is correct and the test expectation is the wrong
side.** The committed assertion demands the host `String`; the Rust target
renders the Haxe `String` representation as its own unit-storage `UString`
everywhere else in the tree. The change is therefore in the test, not the
generator — and the emitted code *cannot* compile with a `String` field
(section 2.7). `rust-f32` carries no parallel assertion; one is added so the
contract is pinned at both precisions.

---

## 1. The mismatch

| Side | Text |
| --- | --- |
| Test (`tests/ts/value-type.test.ts:125`) | `#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]\npub struct FontFaceId(pub String);` |
| Generator output (`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:42`) | `pub struct FontFaceId(pub UString);` |

The source under test is `samples/boring/ValueTypeOps.hx:32`,
`@:valueType abstract FontFaceId(String) from String`, whose representation is
the Haxe `String` type.

## 2. The decision, with evidence

### 2.1 The spec rules the field from the representation

`docs/specs/features/02-abstract-types.md:201` rules the Rust product of a
value wrapper:

> `#[derive(...)] pub struct Name(pub Rep);` with the declared members in an
> inherent `impl` … Derives follow the representation: `PartialEq` always;
> `Eq` and `Hash` only when the representation holds them, so a `Float`
> representation derives `PartialEq` alone.

`Rep` is *the representation*; the same file names this wrapper at
`docs/specs/features/02-abstract-types.md:155-156` and `:231-232` as
"`FontFaceId` (a `String` representation whose constructor rejects blank values
and whose `toString` returns the value)". So the question is not "value wrapper
or not" — it is **what the Rust target renders the Haxe `String` as**.

The spec's own example of a Rust newtype over a representation is the
`CodePoint` candidate at `docs/specs/features/02-abstract-types.md:66-73`
("Rust Candidate 1: Newtype tuple struct"):

```rust
pub struct CodePoint(pub u32);
```

and the ruling at `:140` sends an abstract with explicit constraints to "single-field
newtype structs in Rust". The Haxe representation there is `Int`, and the field
is the *target's* `u32` — the Rust rendering of Haxe `Int` — not the Haxe name.
The representation is rendered through the target's type mapper; the wrapper
adds no spelling of its own.

### 2.2 The Rust target renders Haxe `String` as `UString` / `&UStr`

`packages/compiler/reflaxe/rust/rustcompiler/RustType.hx:176-179` is the single
mapping:

```haxe
case "String":
    imports.requireType("runtime.UString", "UString");
    imports.requireType("runtime.UString", "UStr");
    isParam ? "&UStr" : "UString";
```

The generated runtime states what that type is,
`reference/rust/gen/runtime/u_string.rs:2-4`:

> `// Haxe String storage: owned UTF-16 code units, the Rust target's`
> `// native string type. UString is the owned form, UStr is the borrowed`
> `// slice form, matching the String/&str relationship.`

`UString` is the owned form and `UStr` the borrowed form of the *Haxe* string.
Host Rust `String` is a separate, UTF-8 type reached only through explicit
named conversions (`to_utf8_lossy`, `to_utf8`) declared in the same file.

### 2.3 The field and the "representation" come from the same call

`RustDecl.valueTypeDecl` derives both from one expression
(`packages/compiler/reflaxe/rust/rustcompiler/RustDecl.hx:697` and `:701`):

```haxe
final representation = types.of(info.representation, false);
...
"pub struct " + info.name + "(pub " + representation + ");",
```

`types.of(String, false)` *is* `"UString"` by section 2.2. There is no second
mapper and no value-wrapper-specific string override, so `pub UString` is the
only output this code path can produce. The expectation is not a live
ambiguity in the emitter; it is an assertion that contradicts the emitter.

### 2.4 The surrounding emitted code is `UString`-consistent

`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:42-60` — the *same* declaration the
test reads:

```rust
pub struct FontFaceId(pub UString);              // :42

impl FontFaceId {
    pub fn new(value: &UStr) -> Result<Self, ValueError> {   // :45
        if value.trim() == UString::from("") {               // :46
            return Err(ValueError::BlankValue);
        }
        return Ok(Self(value.to_ustring()));                 // :49
    }

    fn to_string(&self) -> UString {                          // :52
        return self.0.clone();                               // :53
    }
}

impl std::fmt::Display for FontFaceId {                       // :57
    fn fmt(&self, formatter: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        write!(formatter, "{}", self.to_string())             // :59
    }
}
```

These are exactly the emitter's fixed shapes for a string representation:
`RustDecl.hx:711` (`ctorArgType = … "&UStr"`), `:722`
(`ctorValue = … "value.to_ustring()"`), `:763`
(`fn to_string(&self) -> UString`) and `:816` (`Display` via `self.to_string()`).
The wrapper's own constructor parameter borrows `&UStr`, `new` converts the
borrow with `to_ustring()`, `to_string` returns `UString`, and `Display`
delegates to it. **That is the intended contract for a value wrapper's
constructor parameter**, and it is only satisfiable by a `UString` field.

Derives match too: `RustDecl.hx:698` derives `Eq, Hash` for a non-float
representation, and `reference/rust/gen/runtime/u_string.rs:15` gives
`UString` `PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash` while `:213` gives it
`Display`. (`Ic`'s `f64` representation correspondingly derives only
`PartialEq`, as the test itself asserts at `:120-121`.)

The consumer module closes the loop —
`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_consumer.rs:66-68`:

```rust
pub fn value_type_consumer_rendered_id() -> UString {
    let id = FontFaceId::new(UStr::new(&[102,97,99,101])).unwrap();
    return UString::from((id).to_string().as_str());
}
```

The call site passes a borrowed `&UStr` and the function returns `UString`.
A `String` field would make `new` return a wrapper whose accessor disagrees
with every call site in the tree.

### 2.5 Sibling generated code uses the same shape

`FontFaceId` is the only `@:valueType` abstract with a `String` representation,
so the comparable declarations are the ordinary ones with a Haxe `String`
component. They are unanimous:

| Site | Text |
| --- | --- |
| `reference/rust/gen/boring/clone_derive_gap_ops.rs:27`, `:31`, `:34` | `pub label: UString`, `pub fn new(range: …, label: &UStr)`, `label: label.to_ustring()` |
| `reference/rust/gen/boring/font_id.rs:9` (sibling abstract over `String`) | `pub fn font_id_impl_of(face: &UStr) -> UString` |
| `reference/rust/gen/boring/*.rs` (survey) | 46 `pub <name>: UString` fields, 5 `pub <name>: String` fields — all five are the hardcoded `*Fault` message-only exception shape of section 2.6; 0 `pub struct X(pub String)` newtypes |

The emitter's own comment states the contract verbatim
(`RustDecl.hx:2401-2402`): "The unit-storage twin borrows as `&UStr` while the
field owns a `UString`; `to_ustring()` converts the borrow."

### 2.6 Host `String` appears in exactly one hardcoded place — a boundary

The only host-`String` fields in the generated tree belong to the message-only
exception structs, and those declarations are hand-written rather than mapped
(`RustDecl.hx:901-906`) — all five of them, e.g.:

```haxe
return ["#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]", "pub struct " + name + " {", "    pub message: String,", "}",
    "impl " + name + " {", "    pub fn new(message: &str) -> Self {", "        Self { message: message.to_string() }", …
```

The construction sites of these structs convert explicitly at the boundary
(`RustExpr.hx:2355-2367`):

> `// The message-only constructor keeps a std &str slot (the payload enum`
> `// renders its message with format!), so a literal stays a raw str literal`
> `// and every other string expression converts to UTF-8 once at the boundary.`

and host `&str` slots generally are confined to native interfaces
(`RustExpr.hx:2500-2503`: "a native `&str` slot (an enum `from_name` lookup,
the runtime `Fs` module) takes std UTF-8 text (no UStr view)"). So the
appearance of host `String`/`&str` in this compiler is a *deliberate, explicit*
boundary conversion — the opposite of the value wrapper, where no conversion
exists and the accessors are typed `UString`.

### 2.7 Empirical: the emitted code compiles; the test's expectation cannot

This is the decisive check, run on the scratch root (both precisions):

| Tree | As shipped (`pub UString`) | With the test's `pub String` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `reference/rust/gen` (`cargo check -p boring-codec-gen --all-targets`) | `rc=0` | `rc=101`, **2 × `error[E0308]: mismatched types`** |
| `reference/rust-f32/gen` (`cargo check --manifest-path reference/rust-f32/gen/Cargo.toml --all-targets`) | `rc=0` | `rc=101`, **2 × `error[E0308]: mismatched types`** |

The two errors are exactly the two places section 2.4 predicted
(`reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs`):

```
error[E0308]: mismatched types
  --> reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:49:24
49 |         return Ok(Self(value.to_ustring()));
   |                   ---- ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ expected `String`, found `UString`

error[E0308]: mismatched types
  --> reference/rust/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:53:16
52 |     fn to_string(&self) -> UString {
   |                            ------- expected `UString` because of return type
53 |         return self.0.clone();
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ expected `UString`, found `String`
```

Raw logs: `evidence/gen-diff/cargo-check-mutated-String.log`,
`evidence/gen-diff/cargo-check-f32-as-shipped.log`,
`evidence/gen-diff/cargo-check-f32-mutated-String.log`. The mutation is confined
to that one field and was reverted immediately; the restored file's sha256
matches the recorded `evidence/gen-diff/as-shipped.sha256`.

**If `String` were the required spelling, the committed generated tree — the
fixture every other generated-tree test reads — would not compile.** That is
not a tenable reading of the contract.

### 2.8 The counter-evidence is a stale pre-migration row

Two documents still carry the old mapping:

- `docs/specs/features/14-type-system-mapping.md:162` — `` | `String` | `String` or `&str` | `string` | `String` | ``
- `docs/tutorial.md:311` — the same row.

They are leftovers, not rulings, for a checkable reason: the mapping row
predates the unit-storage change. The change landed in
`d0ffa110 "spike(rust): measure the fallout of a unit-based string type"`
(2026-09-25), which touched only
`Compiler.hx`, `RustExpr.hx`, `RustRuntime.hx` and `RustType.hx` — no documents.
The two rows were last written by `d15ee1ed` and `f4198311`, both earlier;
`docs/specs/features/14-type-system-mapping.md` contains **0** occurrences of
`UString`. Nothing enforces the row: `tools/type-mapping/check.ts` validates
only the `` `Int` `` row of that table.

Three further asymmetries make "the generator is wrong" implausible:

1. The unit-storage machinery is an adopted subsystem, not a stray emitter
   branch: ~550 lines of `USTRING_TYPE_SOURCE` / `USTRING_ABI_SOURCE_NEW` in
   `RustRuntime.hx`, a shipped `reference/rust/gen/runtime/u_string.rs`, and
   `docs/specs/stdlib/10-unicode-string-access.md:97-102`,
   `docs/specs/stdlib/15-string-unit-operations.md:37-39,58` and
   `docs/specs/features/08-strings-and-unicode.md:217-218` all rule Rust
   lowerings onto `u_string::*` for Haxe `String`. The unit storage is also the
   only way to satisfy `docs/specs/features/54-stringbuf-unpaired-surrogate-rulings.md`
   (Rust `&str` cannot carry an unpaired surrogate).
2. Blast radius: `reference/rust/gen` and `reference/rust-f32/gen` contain 20+
   `UString` fields and hundreds of `UString` function signatures. Reverting the
   mapping would break dozens of tests; the current state breaks exactly one
   assertion.
3. The test suite agrees with the generator everywhere else: 61 `UString`
   occurrences across 16 test files, versus exactly **one** assertion anywhere
   in `tests/` that expects host `String` in a generated tree — the line at
   issue (`evidence/test-assertion-audit.txt`).

The same assertion is also the only Rust spelling in that test that was
generalised from the other targets: `export type FontFaceId = string` (`:90`)
is right for TypeScript and `FontFaceId makeFontFaceId(String value)` (`:117`)
is right for Dart, because on those targets the Haxe string *is* the host
string. Rust is the one target with a distinct Haxe-string type, and the
expectation copied the host spelling into it.

## 3. The change

`tests/ts/value-type.test.ts` only — two hunks, no generator change
(`PATCH.diff`):

1. `:125` — the expected field becomes `pub UString`, with a short comment
   recording why, so the spelling is not "fixed" back.
2. The `rust-f32` block gains the parallel assertion. `rust-f32` had **no**
   `FontFaceId` assertion at all, so it was not mismatched — it was unasserted.
   Its generated file is byte-identical to the f64 tree except in float width
   (`Ic(pub f32)` / `f32` parameters); the `FontFaceId` declaration is identical
   (`reference/rust-f32/gen/boring/value_type_ops.rs:42`). Adding the assertion
   pins the contract at both precisions and removes the asymmetry that let the
   stale spelling survive.

The test file is at its committed revision in the shared tree
(`git diff --stat -- tests/ts/value-type.test.ts` is empty), so the patch
applies to `e1c65975` cleanly (`patch -p1 --dry-run` verified).

